Z||000000||overlord||02-01-2000||05:02 PM||||2.5 2m4 (engine is newer  87 i think)<P>the past month or so..i have been getting poor gas mileage (seems after i put new tires on)<P>the enigne runs great (except the timing needs to be set)<BR>and no major problems that i know of.<P>can someone make a list of what could cause poor gas mileage.<BR>and maybe i can figure this out.thanx||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||02-01-2000||05:24 PM||||Are your new tires a significantly different size that the old ones?<P>The timing being off will hurt MPG.<P>I see you're in Canada. Living in the northern US the last few years, I have noticed my MPG will drop every winter on every car I've owned. I think it is a combination of the "winter mix" the oil companies use, more time spent idling warming up and more time spent driving while the engine is not fully warmed up.<P>What mileage are you getting and under what driving conditions?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||02-01-2000||05:31 PM||||overlord, <P>The 87-88 2.5L is easy to differentiate from earlier models.  If it does not have a distributor.  Otherwise you have a 2.5L from another car or other years.  For the record, my 87 SC drops severly in mileage during cold months.  This is primarily due to the Dukes poor cold start up behavior.  From what I understand this is a problem that has always plagued the engine.  Ogre knows quite a bit about this engine... perhaps he will see the thread tonight.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000003||tiacPaul||02-01-2000||06:14 PM||||I live in Dayton Ohio and have a duke.  Mine has been fine except it needs an extra second to start, and warm up about three min. Can't help much on the milage because my odomiter stopped when it got cold.  Definately do the timeing and try routine things like grease the chasis and gas treatments.||||reg||1
